('use_of_english_0320', 'Use of English', 'Idiomatic expressions', 'Sentence Interpretation (Idiom)', NULL, '[{"weight": 1, "conceptId": "91ez3x"}]'::jsonb, '{}', 2, 'application', 60, 'low', 'none', '[{"option": "A", "explanation": "Parrying means evading, the opposite of answering brilliantly.", "misconceptionId": "confused_similar_concepts"}, {"option": "C", "explanation": "Parrying is a skilful deflection, not a failure to answer.", "misconceptionId": "misunderstood_terminology"}, {"option": "D", "explanation": "Parrying avoids direct engagement, so it doesn''t mean mastering the questions.", "misconceptionId": "confused_similar_concepts"}]'::jsonb, '{}', 'jamb_past_question', 2005, 'JAMB', '{}', 'Select the option that best explains the information conveyed in the sentence.

The Governor parried all the questions put to him by the journalist?', '[{"label": "A", "text": "The governor answered all the questions  brilliantly", "isCorrect": false}, {"label": "B", "text": "The Governor evaded all the questions", "isCorrect": true}, {"label": "C", "text": "The Governor failed all the questions", "isCorrect": false}, {"label": "D", "text": "The  Governor mastered all the questions", "isCorrect": false}]'::jsonb, 'B', 'To ''parry'' a question means to skilfully evade or deflect it, so the Governor evaded all the questions.', 'live', '{"totalAttempts": 0, "correctCount": 0, "avgResponseTimeMs": 0, "distractorSelectionCounts": {}}'::jsonb),
('use_of_english_0321', 'Use of English', 'Synonyms/antonyms', 'Antonyms', NULL, '[{"weight": 1, "conceptId": "v4agon"}]'::jsonb, '{}', 2, 'recall', 60, 'low', 'none', '[{"option": "A", "explanation": "''Ordinary and uninteresting'' is a synonym of workaday, not its opposite.", "misconceptionId": "misunderstood_terminology"}, {"option": "C", "explanation": "''Comfortable'' isn''t the dimension workaday addresses; this shifts the meaning rather than reversing it.", "misconceptionId": "confused_similar_concepts"}, {"option": "D", "explanation": "''Ugly and unfascinating'' intensifies workaday''s meaning rather than opposing it.", "misconceptionId": "misunderstood_terminology"}]'::jsonb, '{}', 'jamb_past_question', 2005, 'JAMB', '{}', 'Choose the option opposite in meaning to the underlined word(s).

